\title{SCAtools: Direction-Aware Sufficient Condition Analysis}
\description{
Tests whether a high or low level of a condition is enough to guarantee a high or low level of an outcome. Such a claim predicts that one corner of the condition-outcome scatter plot is empty of observations. The package estimates the boundary of that empty corner, its size as a share of the scope (the effect size), a permutation test against random pairing, and a threshold table giving the condition level that guarantees each outcome level, in actual units, percentages, percentiles or standard deviations. Each of the four directions (high or low condition, high or low outcome) is stated as a claim and mapped to its empty corner. Because a condition can rule out low outcomes while barely moving the average, results can be set beside ordinary least-squares and median regression slopes, showing whether the data hold an average effect, a sufficiency effect or both. Also provides plots, influential-case diagnostics, scope-sensitivity and resampling checks, random-data generation and power analysis for planning studies. Frontiers are estimated with the 'NCA' package.
}
\section{The method}{

A condition is sufficient for an outcome when the condition guarantees the
outcome. In a scatter plot of condition against outcome, such a claim rules
out one corner: high X sufficient for high Y, for instance, leaves no cases
with high X and low Y. Necessary condition analysis (NCA; Dul, 2016)
estimates the empty corner of a necessity claim. By contraposition a
sufficiency claim is a necessity claim about the opposite levels -- high X
sufficient for high Y is low X necessary for low Y -- so its empty corner
can be estimated with the same frontier techniques. SCAtools states the four
sufficiency directions as claims, maps each to the corner it leaves empty,
and reports the effect size, permutation test and sufficiency thresholds in
the vocabulary of condition analysis in degree, following Chan (in press),
\emph{From Necessity to Sufficiency and Necessity-and-Sufficiency: Extending
Condition Analysis in Degree}.
}

\section{What it is for}{

\itemize{
\item Finding the condition level that guarantees each outcome level,
reported in actual units, percentages, percentiles or standard
deviations (\code{\link[=sca_thresholds]{sca_thresholds()}}).
\item Measuring an effect that regression does not show: a condition can
rule out low outcomes while barely moving the average. \code{\link[=sca_compare]{sca_compare()}}
reports the two side by side.
\item Comparing several conditions, each with its own direction, in one
table (\code{\link[=sca_table]{sca_table()}}).
\item Planning a study: \code{\link[=sca_power]{sca_power()}} estimates the sample size a
sufficiency effect of a given size needs.
}
}

\section{How to use it}{

\enumerate{
\item Fit the claim with \code{\link[=sca_analysis]{sca_analysis()}}, naming the condition, the
outcome and the direction, and the scope if theory gives one. Use
\code{test.rep} for the permutation test; \code{\link[=sca]{sca()}} runs 1000 permutations and
draws the plot in one step.
\item Read the results with \code{\link[=sca_table]{sca_table()}}, \code{\link[=sca_thresholds]{sca_thresholds()}} and
\code{\link[=sca_plot]{sca_plot()}}; \code{\link[=sca_output]{sca_output()}} prints and draws them together.
\item Set the result beside regression with \code{\link[=sca_compare]{sca_compare()}} and
\code{\link[=sca_locationshift]{sca_locationshift()}}.
\item Check it with \code{\link[=sca_test_plot]{sca_test_plot()}}, \code{\link[=sca_scope]{sca_scope()}},
\code{\link[=sca_scope_sensitivity]{sca_scope_sensitivity()}}, \code{\link[=sca_resample]{sca_resample()}} and \code{\link[=sca_outliers]{sca_outliers()}}.
